module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjGradedIdeal.ClosedSubscheme
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjGradedIdeal.VanishingPresentation

/-!
# A closed subscheme of a relative `Proj` is cut out by a finite-type submodule of one degree

`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/RelativeProjGradedIdeal/ClosedSubscheme.lean` proves Tag 0802 from
a `…RelativeProjGradedIdeal.GradedQuotientPresentation` — the relative case of Tag 0801 — carried
as an argument, because that file is upstream of the construction of the presentation:
`…RelativeProjGradedIdeal.compactSpace_proj_grading`, which it proves, is what makes a chart of a
quasi-compact relative `Proj` quasi-compact, and the construction of the presentation needs it.

This file supplies the argument. `…RelativeProjTwists.gradedQuotientPresentation` is the
presentation, built with no hypothesis beyond Tag 0802's own, so the statement below is the whole
of Tag 0802. A sibling file does the same for Tag 0803, whose argument is the
eight-field `…RelativeProjGradedIdeal.VanishingIdealPresentation`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjTwists.exists_finiteType_degreeQuotientPresentation`:
  **Tag 0802.**

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01NN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01NN)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0801](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0801)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0802](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0802)

## Implementation notes

The statement is separated from
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/RelativeProjGradedIdeal/VanishingPresentation.lean` because that
file needs `TopCat.Sheaf` and `CategoryTheory.sheafCompose` made `implicit_reducible` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.top` made `instance_reducible` — without which the chart
identification `…RelativeProjTwists.ChartData.chartIso` cannot be elaborated at all — and those
are not wanted here.

The single-degree form of Tag 0801 that the conclusion names is not a second argument either:
`…RelativeProjGradedIdeal.degreeQuotientPresenter_of_gradedQuotientPresentation` derives it from
the graded-quotient presentation, inside the statement this file wraps.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry TopologicalSpace Opposite
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProj
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjBridge
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjGradedIdeal

namespace Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjTwists

universe u

variable {S : Scheme.{u}}

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 0802.** A closed subscheme of finite presentation of a quasi-compact
relative `Proj` over a quasi-compact quasi-separated base is the relative `Proj` of the quotient
of `𝒜` by the graded ideal generated by a quasi-coherent finite type submodule `ℱ` of a single
homogeneous piece `𝒜_d` with `d > 0`.

The relative case of Tag 0801 — the graded-quotient presentation that
`…RelativeProjGradedIdeal.exists_finiteType_degreeQuotientPresentation` consumes — is
`…RelativeProjTwists.gradedQuotientPresentation`, so the statement carries no hypothesis beyond
the tag's own. -/
@[stacks 0802]
public theorem exists_finiteType_degreeQuotientPresentation (A : SheafGradedAlgebra S)
    {Z : Scheme.{u}} (i : Z ⟶ (relativeProj A).scheme)
    [CompactSpace S] [QuasiSeparatedSpace S] [IsClosedImmersion i]
    [QuasiCompact (relativeProj A).toBase]
    (hi : AlgebraicGeometry.FinitePresentation i) :
    ∃ d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ ∃ ℱ : (A.piece (d : ℤ)).Submodule,
      ℱ.toSheafOfModules.IsQuasicoherent ∧
        SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType.{u, u, u} ℱ.toSheafOfModules ∧
          Nonempty (DegreeQuotientPresentation A i ℱ) :=
  RelativeProjGradedIdeal.exists_finiteType_degreeQuotientPresentation A i hi
    (gradedQuotientPresentation i)

end Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProjTwists

end
